WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.180
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular&nbsp;
Memory—KP here. I’ve done some longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.060
freeform PyMOL tutorials in the past. But in&nbsp;
this series, I’ll take you through shorter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.380
targeted lessons, so it’ll be easy to&nbsp;
get started and find the exact videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:24.000
you need. Let’s begin with basic operation of&nbsp;
the PyMOL 2 Graphical User Interface or GUI. 
&nbsp;

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.940
I’m using PyMOL 2.4.2—if you’re using another&nbsp;
version, you might notice differences. Feel free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.160
to drop your version number and observations&nbsp;
in the comments to help out other learners.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.120
We’ll load a structure of hemoglobin&nbsp;
from the protein data bank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:54.060
Let’s check it out in the RCSB PDB first. Navigate&nbsp;
to rcsb.org and type 4mqk in the search bar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.920
On the page that comes up, notice&nbsp;
the four differently colored parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.160
Hemoglobin is a tetramer, a protein&nbsp;
made up of 4 separate chains.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:12.960
To load the biological assembly of hemoglobin in&nbsp;
PyMOL, click the dropdown file menu, and select&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:24.540
Get PDB. Type 4mqk in the PDB ID box. Now—very&nbsp;
important—type the number 1 into assembly. 
&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.560
Click download and the structure pops&nbsp;
up in the structure viewer window. 
&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.260
We’ll have a whole episode on coloring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:36.720
but for now, click on color, the&nbsp;
C button here, in the names panel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.880
Choose “by chain” and select the top choice.&nbsp;
Notice the four uniquely colored chains.
&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:51.720
A faster way to load structures is to use the&nbsp;
command line, where you can execute actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:59.280
with typed codes. Type fetch, followed&nbsp;
by the four-digit alphanumeric PDB ID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:08.460
But, when we do this with 4mqk, we see 8&nbsp;
chains! Without getting too deep, the fetch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.680
command by itself loads the structure in a way&nbsp;
that’s related to crystal structure symmetry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.840
which will sometimes show extra stuff, and&nbsp;
sometimes display half of the protein! 
&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.740
If you really want to use the command line to&nbsp;
load structures, I’ll show a longer code on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:32.820
that’ll load the biological assembly. I learned&nbsp;
this from the PyMOL Wiki, your one-stop-shop for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.620
powerful command line codes that’ll help&nbsp;
you do lots of cool stuff with PyMOL. 

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.540
OK, main takeaways: 
1. Use GET PDB in the file menu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:48.540
to load structure, and set the assembly to 1
2. Compare your structure to the image in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.680
the RCSB PDB = to make sure it loads as expected
&nbsp;

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.480
3. Visit the PyMOL Wiki to&nbsp;
learn command line codes

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:04.440
The whole point of using a structure viewer like&nbsp;
PyMOL is to get a sense of the molecule in three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:12.060
dimensions. PyMOL used to pretty much require&nbsp;
a mouse, and you may still prefer to use one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:18.840
but updates to the GUI have made it really easy&nbsp;
to use PyMOL with a trackpad and that’s what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.580
I’ll show in this tutorial.
Rotate is left click and drag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.960
so I’m just clicking and dragging&nbsp;
on my trackpad to rotate here. 
&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:36.420
Zoom is right click—so clicking and running two&nbsp;
fingers up and down the trackpad zooms in and out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:41.460
More conveniently, the pinch and&nbsp;
spread motion also zooms. 
&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.700
Sometimes we’ll want to move the&nbsp;
whole structure to reposition it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:54.780
This is a trackpad/keyboard combo. On my&nbsp;
Mac, I can click either command or option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.660
and then click and drag using the trackpad,&nbsp;
and this translates the whole molecule. 
&nbsp;

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:09.240
To recenter, click the Action button—the little&nbsp;
A here in the names panel—and select center. 

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:16.560
The first thing I do after loading a structure is&nbsp;
show the sequence. In the dropdown menu, select&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:24.600
DISPLAY, sequence. The sequence pops up, giving&nbsp;
the one letter codes for the amino acids that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:32.400
make up the primary structure of the protein. 
This gray bar here is a scroll bar. Let’s look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:39.180
the whole sequence by clicking and dragging on&nbsp;
it. Following the first chain, we find a string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:46.980
of Os—the oxygen atoms in water molecules, which&nbsp;
are red dots in the structure viewer here. 
&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.540
Continue scrolling and we find Chain B. Notice&nbsp;
it is colored the same as in the structure below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:02.280
making it easy to identify. If we scroll further,&nbsp;
we’ll see some unique three letter codes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:10.500
HEM is the heme molecule that shows up in a stick&nbsp;
representation in the structure viewer below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:19.260
Heme enables hemoglobin to bind oxygen. We can&nbsp;
select one of the hemes by clicking on it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.700
the sequence here, or on the structure&nbsp;
itself in the structure viewer. 
&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:34.140
There’s also CMO. These are carbon monoxide&nbsp;
molecules bound to the oxygen binding site.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:41.100
They’re smaller so they’re hard to see, so let’s&nbsp;
use PyMOL to focus on one of them. Click on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:50.280
CMO in the sequence. The letters sele pop up in&nbsp;
the names panel, and this selection corresponds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:57.000
to whatever we have selected. Click the&nbsp;
A button for actions, and choose zoom. 

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:01.560
It zooms in really close!&nbsp;
I’ll zoom out just a bit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:09.060
Notice how parts of hemoglobin appear chopped&nbsp;
off. This is because zoom applies clipping to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.280
the structure. Clipping shaves away layers&nbsp;
of the molecule so that whatever you zoomed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:19.800
in on can be seen easily. Adjust the&nbsp;
clipping by running two fingers up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:26.160
or down on the trackpad. Clipping can also be&nbsp;
adjusted using the dropdown DISPLAY menu. 
&nbsp;

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:36.120
Let’s rotate the molecule a bit by clicking and&nbsp;
dragging. We can see the carbon monoxide binds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.680
near the HEME in hemoglobin. Carbon&nbsp;
monoxide binds well to hemoglobin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:46.560
and can prevent oxygen binding. This is the&nbsp;
basis for carbon monoxide poisoning. 
&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.800
And that’s today’s lesson! Join me next time&nbsp;
to learn how to save selections and use them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.780
to display different parts of the structure&nbsp;
in different ways. Thanks for watching.

